1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,833
A light microscope cannot
show you a single virus.

2
00:00:03,833 --> 00:00:05,724
An electron microscope can.

3
00:00:06,125 --> 00:00:08,736
Ripples on a pond bend around a stone.

4
00:00:08,736 --> 00:00:10,573
A cricket ball never does.

5
00:00:10,973 --> 00:00:14,306
Aarav saw a virus photo on
the college notice board.

6
00:00:14,306 --> 00:00:16,730
Made with electrons, not with light.

7
00:00:17,080 --> 00:00:19,762
How can a tiny particle take a picture?

8
00:00:19,762 --> 00:00:22,544
Because a moving electron is also a wave.

9
00:00:23,044 --> 00:00:27,274
By the end, you will find the wavelength
of anything that moves, from an

10
00:00:27,274 --> 00:00:28,987
electron to a cricket ball.

11
00:00:29,637 --> 00:00:32,556
In the last episode,
light showed two faces.

12
00:00:32,556 --> 00:00:34,839
A wave, and a stream of photons.

13
00:00:35,189 --> 00:00:39,208
In nineteen twenty four, Louis de
Broglie turned the idea around.

14
00:00:39,608 --> 00:00:44,498
If light, a wave, can act like
particles, then particles like electrons

15
00:00:44,498 --> 00:00:46,023
can act like waves.

16
00:00:46,623 --> 00:00:49,368
He gave every moving
particle a wavelength.

17
00:00:49,368 --> 00:00:51,178
We call it a matter wave.

18
00:00:51,778 --> 00:00:54,132
Let's watch it on our molecule bench.

19
00:00:54,132 --> 00:00:56,877
Here is an electron, moving to the right.

20
00:00:57,227 --> 00:00:59,219
Its matter wave travels with it.

21
00:00:59,219 --> 00:01:01,760
Crest to crest is its wavelength, lambda.

22
00:01:02,160 --> 00:01:03,385
Now speed it up.

23
00:01:03,385 --> 00:01:05,083
The crests squeeze closer.

24
00:01:05,083 --> 00:01:06,876
The wavelength gets shorter.

25
00:01:07,276 --> 00:01:10,084
Slow it down, and the
wave stretches out again.

26
00:01:10,484 --> 00:01:13,901
Now give it four times the
mass, at the same speed.

27
00:01:13,901 --> 00:01:15,475
The wave shrinks again.

28
00:01:15,475 --> 00:01:17,836
Wavelength depends on mass and speed.

29
00:01:18,486 --> 00:01:21,874
Let's build de Broglie's
relation, one step at a time.

30
00:01:22,224 --> 00:01:23,124
Step one.

31
00:01:23,118 --> 00:01:26,596
Momentum is mass times
velocity, p equals m v.

32
00:01:26,596 --> 00:01:29,921
It tells how hard a
moving thing is to stop.

33
00:01:30,671 --> 00:01:31,571
Step two.

34
00:01:31,504 --> 00:01:34,640
For a photon, the
momentum is h over lambda.

35
00:01:34,640 --> 00:01:36,254
h is Planck's constant.

36
00:01:37,004 --> 00:01:37,913
Step three.

37
00:01:37,913 --> 00:01:41,018
de Broglie said the same
rule works for matter.

38
00:01:41,018 --> 00:01:42,184
Turn it around.

39
00:01:42,184 --> 00:01:43,865
Lambda equals h over p.

40
00:01:44,615 --> 00:01:45,708
Step four.

41
00:01:45,708 --> 00:01:47,730
Put in p equals m v.

42
00:01:47,730 --> 00:01:50,067
Lambda equals h over m v.

43
00:01:50,817 --> 00:01:52,023
Check the units.

44
00:01:52,023 --> 00:01:55,737
Joule seconds over kilogram
metres per second leaves metres.

45
00:01:55,737 --> 00:01:57,707
A length, as it should be.

46
00:01:58,457 --> 00:02:01,269
Now compare two moving
things side by side.

47
00:02:01,269 --> 00:02:03,525
An electron, and a cricket ball.

48
00:02:03,925 --> 00:02:05,025
Pause and think.

49
00:02:05,025 --> 00:02:07,932
Whose wave is long enough
for us to ever notice?

50
00:02:11,132 --> 00:02:12,204
The electron!

51
00:02:12,204 --> 00:02:16,449
Its mass is tiny, so h over
m v is big enough to measure.

52
00:02:16,449 --> 00:02:19,486
The ball's wave is far
too small to detect.

53
00:02:20,136 --> 00:02:21,162
Remember this.

54
00:02:21,162 --> 00:02:23,327
Everything that moves has a wave.

55
00:02:23,327 --> 00:02:25,206
Only the tiny ones show it.

56
00:02:25,906 --> 00:02:27,641
Now, one worked example.

57
00:02:27,941 --> 00:02:32,516
An electron moves at three times
ten to the six metres per second.

58
00:02:32,516 --> 00:02:37,217
Its mass is nine point one zero nine
times ten to the minus thirty one

59
00:02:37,217 --> 00:02:38,117
kilograms.

60
00:02:38,572 --> 00:02:40,598
Find its de Broglie wavelength.

61
00:02:41,098 --> 00:02:42,454
First, momentum.

62
00:02:42,454 --> 00:02:47,144
Mass times speed is two point seven
three three times ten to the minus

63
00:02:47,144 --> 00:02:49,879
twenty four kilogram metres per second.

64
00:02:50,629 --> 00:02:53,005
Then, lambda equals h over p.

65
00:02:53,005 --> 00:02:58,120
Six point six two six times ten to
the minus thirty four, divided by two

66
00:02:58,120 --> 00:03:02,366
point seven three three times
ten to the minus twenty four.

67
00:03:03,116 --> 00:03:07,380
Lambda is two point four two
times ten to the minus ten metres.

68
00:03:07,380 --> 00:03:11,737
Two hundred and forty two picometres,
about the size of an atom.

69
00:03:12,487 --> 00:03:17,391
A cricket ball at forty metres per
second gets about ten to the minus thirty

70
00:03:17,391 --> 00:03:18,392
four metres.

71
00:03:18,392 --> 00:03:20,364
Far too small to ever see.

72
00:03:21,014 --> 00:03:22,834
So was de Broglie right?

73
00:03:22,834 --> 00:03:23,734
Yes.

74
00:03:23,365 --> 00:03:27,401
Experiments showed that an electron
beam undergoes diffraction.

75
00:03:27,802 --> 00:03:32,403
Shine electrons through a thin crystal,
and they spread into rings of bright

76
00:03:32,403 --> 00:03:34,300
and dark, just like waves.

77
00:03:34,700 --> 00:03:37,569
Electron microscopes use this wave nature.

78
00:03:37,569 --> 00:03:42,365
Electron waves are far shorter than
light waves, so they show much finer

79
00:03:42,365 --> 00:03:43,265
detail.

80
00:03:43,714 --> 00:03:45,180
Now, the exam trap.

81
00:03:45,530 --> 00:03:50,235
Many students put the mass in grams,
or the speed in kilometres per hour.

82
00:03:50,635 --> 00:03:52,252
Always convert first.

83
00:03:52,252 --> 00:03:55,670
Mass in kilograms, speed
in metres per second.

84
00:03:55,670 --> 00:03:58,498
Then h over m v comes out in metres.

85
00:03:59,148 --> 00:04:00,899
Where is this asked?

86
00:04:00,899 --> 00:04:06,471
As numericals on lambda equals h over
m v, and as ratio questions comparing

87
00:04:06,471 --> 00:04:07,423
particles.

88
00:04:08,023 --> 00:04:09,278
Exam level now.

89
00:04:09,278 --> 00:04:13,055
An electron and a proton
have the same kinetic energy.

90
00:04:13,055 --> 00:04:17,268
What is the ratio of the electron's
wavelength to the proton's?

91
00:04:17,268 --> 00:04:20,982
The proton is about eighteen
thirty six times heavier.

92
00:04:20,982 --> 00:04:22,290
Pause and solve.

93
00:04:27,690 --> 00:04:29,014
The answer is C.

94
00:04:29,014 --> 00:04:30,380
About forty three.

95
00:04:30,880 --> 00:04:33,829
Kinetic energy is p squared over two m.

96
00:04:33,829 --> 00:04:36,910
So p equals the square root of two m K.

97
00:04:37,410 --> 00:04:41,163
Then lambda equals h over
the square root of two m K.

98
00:04:41,163 --> 00:04:44,324
Same K, so lambda goes as one over root m.

99
00:04:44,824 --> 00:04:48,504
The ratio is root eighteen
thirty six, about forty three.

100
00:04:48,504 --> 00:04:50,296
B forgets the square root.

101
00:04:50,896 --> 00:04:52,355
Here is your recap.

102
00:04:52,355 --> 00:04:53,854
Screenshot this card.

103
00:04:57,054 --> 00:05:00,052
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

104
00:05:00,452 --> 00:05:01,352
One.

105
00:05:00,931 --> 00:05:04,791
Write the de Broglie relation,
and say what each letter means.

106
00:05:05,591 --> 00:05:06,491
Two.

107
00:05:06,097 --> 00:05:09,485
Why do we never notice the
wave of a cricket ball?

108
00:05:10,285 --> 00:05:11,185
Three.

109
00:05:10,939 --> 00:05:14,776
Which experiment showed that
electrons behave like waves?

110
00:05:15,676 --> 00:05:20,717
Next time: can we know exactly where
an electron is, and how fast it moves,

111
00:05:20,717 --> 00:05:21,617
at once?

112
00:05:21,494 --> 00:05:24,639
We find out in Heisenberg's
uncertainty principle.
